The function pointer type for memory allocation callbacks. }}\par
{\s17\sa60\sb30\widctlpar\qj \fs22\cgrid 
This is the function pointer type for memory allocation callbacks. A memory allocation callback function has the following signature: {
\par
\pard\plain \s41\li360\widctlpar\adjustright \shading1000\cbpat8 \f2\fs16\cgrid {\cf18 void}* function_name({\cf18 size_t} size, {\cf18 void}* user)\par
}
\par
This function must return either a memory block at least {\f2 size}  bytes long, or {\f2 NULL}  if allocation failed. Note that not all parts of GLFW handle allocation failures gracefully yet.\par
This function must support being called during {\b glfwInit} but before the library is flagged as initialized, as well as during {\b glfwTerminate} after the library is no longer flagged as initialized.\par
Any memory allocated via this function will be deallocated via the same allocator during library termination or earlier.\par
Any memory allocated via this function must be suitably aligned for any object type. If you are using C99 or earlier, this alignment is platform-dependent but will be the same as what {\f2 malloc}  provides. If you are using C11 or later, this is the value of {\f2 alignof(max_align_t)} .\par
The size will always be greater than zero. Allocations of size zero are filtered out before reaching the custom allocator.\par
If this function returns {\f2 NULL} , GLFW will emit {\b GLFW_OUT_OF_MEMORY}.\par
This function must not call any GLFW function.\par
